1
00:00:00,260 --> 00:00:04,200
A red blood cell's journey. Understanding double circulation through

2
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:06,171
the heart, lungs and body.

3
00:00:06,817 --> 00:00:09,185
Look inside a large vein. Millions of red blood

4
00:00:09,185 --> 00:00:10,974
cells are flowing back towards the heart. Now freeze

5
00:00:10,974 --> 00:00:14,542
them, and follow just one. This cell is low

6
00:00:14,542 --> 00:00:18,627
on oxygen, its destination is unknown, and it has

7
00:00:18,627 --> 00:00:22,477
visited the heart zero times. Can you trace its

8
00:00:22,477 --> 00:00:25,486
complete journey? And how many times will it pass

9
00:00:25,486 --> 00:00:26,728
through the heart?

10
00:00:27,327 --> 00:00:30,297
The journey starts in the body. Our cell leaves

11
00:00:30,297 --> 00:00:33,587
the body tissues and enters the veins. Blood from

12
00:00:33,587 --> 00:00:37,567
the upper body returns through the superior vena cava,

13
00:00:37,567 --> 00:00:39,914
and blood from the lower body returns through the

14
00:00:39,914 --> 00:00:43,431
inferior vena cava. Both routes meet at the right

15
00:00:43,431 --> 00:00:46,089
atrium. This is the cell's first visit to the

16
00:00:46,089 --> 00:00:51,078
heart. So: body, vena cava, right atrium.

17
00:00:51,677 --> 00:00:54,209
Now we are inside the heart. The cell waits

18
00:00:54,209 --> 00:00:57,773
in the right atrium. Between the right atrium and

19
00:00:57,773 --> 00:01:01,470
the right ventricle is the tricuspid valve. The valve

20
00:01:01,470 --> 00:01:05,321
opens, and the cell moves into the right ventricle.

21
00:01:05,321 --> 00:01:08,267
Then the valve closes behind it. If the blood

22
00:01:08,267 --> 00:01:11,210
tries to flow backward, the valve blocks it. This

23
00:01:11,210 --> 00:01:14,737
is one-way flow. Now the cell is in the

24
00:01:14,737 --> 00:01:16,628
right ventricle. Where will this ventricle send the blood?

25
00:01:17,247 --> 00:01:19,609
The right ventricle contracts, and the cell shoots into

26
00:01:19,609 --> 00:01:23,402
the pulmonary artery. You might think an artery always

27
00:01:23,402 --> 00:01:28,223
carries oxygenated blood. But not always: the pulmonary artery

28
00:01:28,223 --> 00:01:31,553
carries deoxygenated blood to the lungs. The route right

29
00:01:31,553 --> 00:01:36,867
ventricle, pulmonary artery, lungs is called pulmonary circulation. The

30
00:01:36,867 --> 00:01:38,698
cell now approaches the lungs.

31
00:01:39,353 --> 00:01:42,046
Inside the lungs, the cell enters a narrow capillary.

32
00:01:42,046 --> 00:01:45,803
Capillaries are microscopic vessels that connect arteries to veins.

33
00:01:45,803 --> 00:01:48,924
Before the lungs, the cell is deoxygenated. In the

34
00:01:48,924 --> 00:01:52,040
lungs, it picks up oxygen, and its status changes

35
00:01:52,040 --> 00:01:55,967
to oxygenated. Now it leaves through the pulmonary vein.

36
00:01:55,967 --> 00:02:00,393
The pulmonary vein carries oxygenated blood, which is another

37
00:02:00,393 --> 00:02:04,693
exception. Remember: an artery leads away from the heart,

38
00:02:04,693 --> 00:02:06,244
and a vein leads towards it.

39
00:02:06,883 --> 00:02:09,360
The cell has returned to the heart, this time

40
00:02:09,360 --> 00:02:13,410
to the left atrium. The pulmonary loop is complete:

41
00:02:13,410 --> 00:02:16,303
right ventricle, lungs, left atrium. This is the cell's

42
00:02:16,303 --> 00:02:19,383
second visit to the heart. Between the left atrium

43
00:02:19,383 --> 00:02:23,243
and the left ventricle is the bicuspid, or mitral,

44
00:02:23,243 --> 00:02:25,933
valve. It opens, and the cell moves into the

45
00:02:25,933 --> 00:02:29,114
left ventricle. Next stop: the whole body.

46
00:02:29,670 --> 00:02:32,630
The left ventricle contracts strongly and pushes the cell

47
00:02:32,630 --> 00:02:36,450
into the aorta. The aorta carries oxygenated blood to

48
00:02:36,450 --> 00:02:41,610
the body: the brain, arms, liver, kidneys and lower

49
00:02:41,610 --> 00:02:44,883
body. The cell travels along the aorta towards the

50
00:02:44,883 --> 00:02:50,301
body. This route, left ventricle, aorta, body, is called systemic circulation.

51
00:02:50,910 --> 00:02:52,718
Now the cell reaches a capillary bed in the

52
00:02:52,718 --> 00:02:56,528
body tissues. It arrives oxygenated, and gives oxygen to

53
00:02:56,528 --> 00:02:59,070
the cells around it. As it leaves, it is

54
00:02:59,070 --> 00:03:03,350
deoxygenated again. The capillaries join up to form veins,

55
00:03:03,350 --> 00:03:06,661
because capillaries connect arteries to veins. The cell returns

56
00:03:06,661 --> 00:03:09,210
through the vena cava to the right atrium, exactly

57
00:03:09,210 --> 00:03:12,101
where it started. One complete journey.

58
00:03:12,670 --> 00:03:14,550
Now let us see why this is called double

59
00:03:14,550 --> 00:03:19,710
circulation. The first loop: heart, lungs, heart. This is

60
00:03:19,710 --> 00:03:24,240
pulmonary circulation. The second loop: heart, body, heart. This

61
00:03:24,240 --> 00:03:30,213
is systemic circulation. In one complete circulation, the blood

62
00:03:30,213 --> 00:03:32,883
passes through the heart twice. Two passes through the

63
00:03:32,883 --> 00:03:37,820
heart: that is double circulation. Pulmonary loop plus systemic

64
00:03:37,820 --> 00:03:40,061
loop equals double circulation.

65
00:03:40,660 --> 00:03:43,380
Your turn. Be the red blood cell. You are

66
00:03:43,380 --> 00:03:45,800
now in the right atrium. A: where do you

67
00:03:45,800 --> 00:03:50,280
go next? B: which vessel takes you to the

68
00:03:50,280 --> 00:03:54,103
lungs? C: after the lungs, which chamber do you

69
00:03:54,103 --> 00:03:57,368
reach first? D: from the left ventricle, which major

70
00:03:57,368 --> 00:04:01,760
vessel carries you to the body? E: why is

71
00:04:01,760 --> 00:04:05,428
it called double circulation? Take your time, and pause

72
00:04:05,428 --> 00:04:07,211
the video if you need to.

73
00:04:18,530 --> 00:04:22,450
Let us check. A: the right ventricle. B: the

74
00:04:22,450 --> 00:04:26,290
pulmonary artery. C: the left atrium. D: the aorta.

75
00:04:26,290 --> 00:04:29,897
E: because the blood passes through the heart twice

76
00:04:29,897 --> 00:04:32,001
in one complete circulation.

77
00:04:32,710 --> 00:04:34,233
Here is the whole journey in one go. From

78
00:04:34,233 --> 00:04:37,530
the body, through the vena cava, into the right

79
00:04:37,530 --> 00:04:41,382
atrium and the right ventricle. Then the pulmonary artery

80
00:04:41,382 --> 00:04:43,794
and the lungs. Then the pulmonary veins, the left

81
00:04:43,794 --> 00:04:47,170
atrium and the left ventricle. Then the aorta, and

82
00:04:47,170 --> 00:04:50,870
back to the body. Remember: right to the lungs,

83
00:04:50,870 --> 00:04:54,350
left to the body. A pulmonary loop plus a

84
00:04:54,350 --> 00:04:57,587
systemic loop is double circulation. Thank you for watching,

85
00:04:57,587 --> 00:04:59,481
and see you in the next lesson.
